TRACE A MACHINE INSTRUCTION BACK TO THE CHOICE THAT PRODUCED IT.

Status: navigational research note, 2026-10-24.
This note does not add a permitted T32 operation, alter the English-to-Thumb
compiler, or claim execution on a physical processor.

THE QUESTION.

Given a specific instruction, memory access, branch or data table, identify
the exact earlier transformation or rule that produced it. A description of
what the instruction means is not yet an explanation of why this particular
implementation chose it.

English Major C works from the machine upward. ComputerScience works from
semantic intent toward a selected architecture. The two perspectives meet
when we can recover both the bottom-up trace and the top-down decision history.

WHERE TO BEGIN HERE.

The current narrowly scoped direct English-to-T32 experiment is indexed in
README.md and examples/unknown-entry-sum/README.md.

The decision to emit assembly directly for this initial experiment is
recorded in "Why the first compiler emits assembly directly.english".
No general-purpose optimizer, universal intermediate representation or ICK
translation step is implied.

The exact meaning of the execution index is in
architecture/armv7-a/t32/Given a machine - objects and execution.english.
The permitted subset of native operations is in
architecture/armv7-a/t32/All and only these initial operations are permitted.english.
That permission grant does not prove that a particular machine or Android ART
run executed any of the described encodings.

RECONSTRUCT THE PATH.

For each observed instruction, recover:

1. The target and exact instruction bytes, decoding and execution-index address.
2. The complete source and generated assembly, with source-to-instruction
   correspondence where possible.
3. The named compiler stage, file, function and revision that chose this form.
4. Other available forms and the rule that selected or rejected them.
5. How to edit the rule or disable or override it; state when no such control
   exists.
6. The independently observed output and the exact evidence that the changed
   choice survived assembling, linking, loading and execution.

Never infer a two-byte instruction merely from a nearby two-byte encoding.
Never call a symbolic disassembly actual executed instructions without evidence
from the corresponding native process. The current first compiler's status and
acceptance limits remain unchanged.

NEIGHBORING DECISION OWNERS.

The ComputerScience lowering decision atlas is at
https://github.com/walnut-burgundy/computer-science/pull/82
and discusses representation choice, architecture selection, and the
source-to-execution inspection contract.

The Idriç semantics and one-step handoff note is at
https://github.com/isomorphisms/Idric/pull/148
and distinguishes intermediate compiler names from physical memory writes.

The direct DEX backend's exact known decision locations are at
https://github.com/isomorphisms/android-NDK/pull/31
and include checking exported functions, lowering to DEX instructions and
choosing DEX encoded widths.

Android ART is a distinct later compiler/runtime. A DEX listing is not
an ARMv7-A T32 listing. Reconstruct what ART did on the named target instead
of attributing its machine instructions to the DEX emitter.

PURPOSE.

The record should let a human locate, understand, change and inspect each
decision. Choosing the fastest possible implementation automatically is
optional and secondary.
